          {
            "title": "History of Past Illness",
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "code": "11348-0",
                  "display": "History of Past illness Narrative"
                }
              ]
            },
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<p>Known hypertensive for 5 years on monotherapy; suboptimal blood pressure control in last 12 months.</p>\n<p>Type 2 diabetes mellitus diagnosed 8 years ago; on metformin 1000 mg BID.</p>\n<p>Active tobacco smoker (~30 pack-years); refused cessation previously.</p>\n<p>No prior myocardial infarction, stroke, or chronic kidney disease.</p>\n<p>Father died of sudden cardiac death at age 60.</p>"
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Clinical Narrative",
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "code": "55112-5",
                  "display": "Document Narrative"
                }
              ]
            },
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<p>A 64-year-old male smoker with hypertension and type 2 diabetes presented to the Emergency Department with 90 minutes of severe substernal chest pressure radiating to the left upper extremity, diaphoresis and progressive dyspnoea on minimal exertion. ECG demonstrated ≥2 mm ST-segment elevation in leads V1–V4, consistent with an acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction. The patient was taken emergently to the cardiac catheterisation laboratory. Coronary angiography revealed a 95% thrombotic occlusion of the proximal left anterior descending (LAD) artery. A drug-eluting stent was successfully deployed, restoring TIMI 3 flow. Peak high-sensitivity troponin I reached 14,200 ng/L. Post-procedural transthoracic echocardiography showed a mildly reduced left ventricular ejection fraction of 45% with hypokinesis of the anterior wall and the interventricular septum. Dual antiplatelet therapy was continued. The patient remained haemodynamically stable, without recurrence of chest pain or arrhythmias, and was deemed suitable for hospital discharge with structured cardiology follow-up and enrolment in the cardiac rehabilitation programme.</p>"
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Plan of Care",
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "code": "18776-5",
                  "display": "Plan of care Note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<p><strong>Follow-up:</strong> Cardiology outpatient clinic in 14 days. Daily home blood-pressure log. Smoking-cessation counselling appointment within 7 days. Cardiac-rehabilitation Phase II starts in 3 weeks. Repeat lipid panel, HbA1c, and renal-function tests in 3 months.</p><p><strong>Warning symptoms (call 112):</strong></p><ul><li>Recurrent chest pain or pressure lasting more than 5 minutes</li><li>Shortness of breath at rest or worsening at night</li><li>Sudden dizziness, fainting or near-fainting</li><li>Sudden swelling of the ankles or legs</li><li>Bleeding that does not stop within 10 minutes (while on antiplatelet therapy)</li><li>Any new neurological symptom (weakness, slurred speech, vision loss)</li></ul>"
            }
          }
